the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at $x=3?$" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330388 1113 #uuid "a89141e2-893c-5b50-aaac-61d6519d661e" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330388 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-3)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3) & =-\\frac{1}{(3)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-3)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{9})=-\\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330390 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330391 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330392 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330393 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330394 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1041 "selection" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330392 13194139535086 true] [17592186330389 1110 #uuid "5ef19cfc-b04b-562d-90ef-48ef719a6ebf" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330390 1034 "-\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535086 true] [17592186330390 1111 #uuid "566ebf73-101d-5a5b-9b09-2b565a3796a8" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330391 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139535086 true] [17592186330391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535086 true] [1